WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.430
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:03.430 --> 00:00:05.350
LANGSTON BELTON: In Marvel
Studios' Black Panther--

00:00:05.350 --> 00:00:08.380
Wakanda Forever, we get to
visit the underwater nation

00:00:08.380 --> 00:00:09.550
of Talokan.

00:00:09.550 --> 00:00:12.160
And to find out how those
incredible scenes were filmed,

00:00:12.160 --> 00:00:15.370
I'm talking to Visual Effects
Supervisor Geoffrey Baumann.

00:00:15.370 --> 00:00:16.430
Geoff, how are you?

00:00:16.430 --> 00:00:17.830
GEOFFREY BAUMANN: I'm
very well, Langston.

00:00:17.830 --> 00:00:18.630
Thanks for having me.

00:00:18.630 --> 00:00:19.690
I appreciate it.

00:00:19.690 --> 00:00:20.857
LANGSTON BELTON:
Great to have you.

00:00:20.857 --> 00:00:23.590
Now, I heard that for the
underwater scenes in the film,

00:00:23.590 --> 00:00:25.780
you actually shot underwater.

00:00:25.780 --> 00:00:29.153
Now, why did the team choose
to take on that challenge?

00:00:29.153 --> 00:00:30.820
GEOFFREY BAUMANN: One of
the most important things

00:00:30.820 --> 00:00:34.960
in Ryan Coogler's storytelling
is the authenticity

00:00:34.960 --> 00:00:36.550
of where characters come from.

00:00:36.550 --> 00:00:39.250
And I think part of
that here is combining

00:00:39.250 --> 00:00:40.840
an underwater world
with a culture

00:00:40.840 --> 00:00:43.090
that we can all relate to.

00:00:43.090 --> 00:00:46.000
Early on, Ryan had made it
clear that he wanted to shoot

00:00:46.000 --> 00:00:47.590
as much as possible underwater.

00:00:47.590 --> 00:00:50.110
Now, there's
logistical challenges

00:00:50.110 --> 00:00:52.210
that come along with
that process, for sure,

00:00:52.210 --> 00:00:55.900
not just from the technical
side of equipment, tanks and all

00:00:55.900 --> 00:00:59.080
that stuff as well, but also
the abilities of the talent

00:00:59.080 --> 00:00:59.890
as well.

00:00:59.890 --> 00:01:02.230
Are the performers comfortable
with going underwater?

00:01:02.230 --> 00:01:03.730
Not only are they
comfortable, are they still

00:01:03.730 --> 00:01:04.989
able to perform underwater?

00:01:04.989 --> 00:01:07.420
Is Ryan able to give
direction underwater?

00:01:07.420 --> 00:01:10.660
It was quite a challenge, both
due to the physics of being

00:01:10.660 --> 00:01:14.020
underwater, as well
as the visual aspects

00:01:14.020 --> 00:01:16.900
of storytelling, of color
and cameras underwater.

00:01:16.900 --> 00:01:20.830
So it was a big challenge to
show strength, movement, speed,

00:01:20.830 --> 00:01:22.390
and all of those
aspects underwater,

00:01:22.390 --> 00:01:26.500
while at the same time staying
true to MCU expectations,

00:01:26.500 --> 00:01:27.520
if you will.

00:01:27.520 --> 00:01:30.220
LANGSTON BELTON: The underwater
performances are next level.

00:01:30.220 --> 00:01:31.930
How did the cast prepare?

00:01:31.930 --> 00:01:34.120
GEOFFREY BAUMANN: We often
shot, whenever possible,

00:01:34.120 --> 00:01:36.640
an entire scene underwater
with our performers

00:01:36.640 --> 00:01:38.260
after they've gone
through rigorous training

00:01:38.260 --> 00:01:40.420
with our stunt department in
order to hold their breath

00:01:40.420 --> 00:01:43.330
for a multitude of minutes,
three, four, or five,

00:01:43.330 --> 00:01:46.060
six minute long breath
holds in a tank for them

00:01:46.060 --> 00:01:48.040
to understand how
to move in water.

00:01:48.040 --> 00:01:50.200
And the thought was even
if we did then transition

00:01:50.200 --> 00:01:52.540
into a dry for wet world,
there would be a little bit

00:01:52.540 --> 00:01:54.910
of muscle memory that
was there for how

00:01:54.910 --> 00:01:56.980
they had to move around.

00:01:56.980 --> 00:02:00.070
Chris Denison, our
underwater stunt coordinator,

00:02:00.070 --> 00:02:02.110
and our marine team
and the safety team

00:02:02.110 --> 00:02:05.740
did an amazing job of
making all of the performers

00:02:05.740 --> 00:02:07.160
feel comfortable in water.

00:02:07.160 --> 00:02:10.180
It was a multiple-week-long
process of them just

00:02:10.180 --> 00:02:12.550
coming to the tank,
hanging out at the surface,

00:02:12.550 --> 00:02:14.230
learning how to do
their breath holds.

00:02:14.230 --> 00:02:18.220
It's methodical but it's also
somewhat meditation in a way,

00:02:18.220 --> 00:02:19.810
where you need to
kind of really get

00:02:19.810 --> 00:02:23.740
your mind to relax and be at
ease as you hold your breath.

00:02:23.740 --> 00:02:25.240
LANGSTON BELTON:
So once the actors

00:02:25.240 --> 00:02:26.950
were ready to go, how
did the production

00:02:26.950 --> 00:02:28.850
team go about shooting
the underwater scenes?

00:02:28.850 --> 00:02:30.550
GEOFFREY BAUMANN: We did
the previews ahead of time

00:02:30.550 --> 00:02:32.800
to try to figure out what
the angles that Ryan wanted.

00:02:32.800 --> 00:02:35.680
And then we would try to take
the performers through each

00:02:35.680 --> 00:02:38.590
of their scenes with
a small set piece

00:02:38.590 --> 00:02:41.560
that was kind of the center
of the story and shoot that.

00:02:41.560 --> 00:02:43.090
And then that
would be assembled.

00:02:43.090 --> 00:02:46.060
And then we would often then go
back into a dry-for-wet world

00:02:46.060 --> 00:02:49.240
and then repeat the same
process, knowing what it looked

00:02:49.240 --> 00:02:50.890
like in the wet-for-wet world.

00:02:50.890 --> 00:02:53.230
The performers were
aware of how they moved.

00:02:53.230 --> 00:02:55.690
But the dry-for-wet
world then allowed

00:02:55.690 --> 00:03:00.160
both Ryan and Autumn, our DP,
to light it the way they wanted.

00:03:00.160 --> 00:03:01.990
So there was then
the ability for them

00:03:01.990 --> 00:03:05.110
to be able to interact with
the performers in a way

00:03:05.110 --> 00:03:08.200
that you don't necessarily
have underwater.

00:03:08.200 --> 00:03:11.350
When Namor tells the
Talokanil that they're

00:03:11.350 --> 00:03:14.290
going to go attack Wakanda,
the dry-for-wet world

00:03:14.290 --> 00:03:15.400
was his throne room.

00:03:15.400 --> 00:03:18.880
And it was essentially a backing
that the art department had

00:03:18.880 --> 00:03:20.890
built, knowing that
we probably had

00:03:20.890 --> 00:03:26.590
to replace a bunch of the of
the set, costumes, et cetera.

00:03:26.590 --> 00:03:28.900
LANGSTON BELTON: So with so many
visual complexities of water,

00:03:28.900 --> 00:03:30.670
how did the team
apply visual effects

00:03:30.670 --> 00:03:32.920
to create the underwater
look of these scenes?

00:03:32.920 --> 00:03:36.010
GEOFFREY BAUMANN: We needed
a base, a baseline for us

00:03:36.010 --> 00:03:37.990
to understand what
water looked like.

00:03:37.990 --> 00:03:40.780
It's a place that some
of us are familiar with.

00:03:40.780 --> 00:03:42.700
But in the depths
of the sea, it's

00:03:42.700 --> 00:03:46.210
still a very unfamiliar
world for most of us.

00:03:46.210 --> 00:03:49.180
We tried to really, again,
jump back to those early tests

00:03:49.180 --> 00:03:52.630
and use those as our guide
for what water should be like

00:03:52.630 --> 00:03:55.840
and what the lighting of the CG
world should be like as well.

00:03:55.840 --> 00:03:58.270
We started playing
with how light travels

00:03:58.270 --> 00:04:01.330
and what colors Autumn liked
and Ryan liked underwater

00:04:01.330 --> 00:04:02.860
so that we could
try to stay true,

00:04:02.860 --> 00:04:04.720
again, to the reality of water.

00:04:04.720 --> 00:04:06.520
The piece that I'm
most proud of is

00:04:06.520 --> 00:04:09.880
that we were able to include as
many characteristics of water

00:04:09.880 --> 00:04:14.260
as possible so turbidity,
marine snow that affect clarity

00:04:14.260 --> 00:04:17.890
so that it isn't crystal clear,
but at the same time finding

00:04:17.890 --> 00:04:20.769
a balance so that there is
focus, so that as a viewer,

00:04:20.769 --> 00:04:22.150
you're immersed in this world.

00:04:22.150 --> 00:04:24.370
And you understand you're
underwater without having

00:04:24.370 --> 00:04:25.630
to process it too much.

00:04:25.630 --> 00:04:28.240
But yet at the same time,
there is a clear point

00:04:28.240 --> 00:04:29.830
of focus for each shot.

00:04:29.830 --> 00:04:32.440
And that was something that Ryan
really wanted to stay true to,

00:04:32.440 --> 00:04:35.110
of color absorption
and the colors

00:04:35.110 --> 00:04:36.580
that disappear underwater.

00:04:36.580 --> 00:04:40.360
And I think because we did that
early on, it allowed us in post

00:04:40.360 --> 00:04:42.640
to continue to sit with
the studio and the rest

00:04:42.640 --> 00:04:44.530
of the filmmakers
to kind of show what

00:04:44.530 --> 00:04:46.060
underwater truly looked like.

00:04:46.060 --> 00:04:47.440
And I think that
that was something

00:04:47.440 --> 00:04:49.660
that I believe for the most
part we were able to deliver

00:04:49.660 --> 00:04:51.577
on pretty successfully.

00:04:51.577 --> 00:04:52.510
LANGSTON BELTON: I'd say so.

00:04:52.510 --> 00:04:54.490
You and the team did
an incredible job.

00:04:54.490 --> 00:04:56.800
Geoff, thank you so much
for chatting with us.

00:04:56.800 --> 00:04:59.020
And all of you at home,
make sure to rewatch

00:04:59.020 --> 00:05:00.910
every moment of
underwater action

00:05:00.910 --> 00:05:02.780
in Marvel Studios'
Black Panther--

00:05:02.780 --> 00:05:06.950
Wakanda Forever, coming
soon exclusively to Disney+.

00:05:06.950 --> 00:05:10.000
[MUSIC PLAYING]

